t  is already a looming global food crisis  my sense is that this is going to become  a bigger and bigger part of the sort of  global conversation about ukraine in the  coming months but ed what's your sense  of that  well i think the first thing to to think  about is how important uh russia and  ukraine are to  to the food system countries about 800  million people depend upon them for a  large share of their uh if their imports  so for instance about 80 percent um of  egypt's grain comes from  ukraine uh and russia and that 100  million people in egypt there are 90  million people in congo and about 70  percent of their grain comes from  ukraine and russia so these are these  are very large numbers so the dependency  is clearly there  and then um i think the war hits the  supply in three ways it hits it directly  because  uh grain and oils sunflower oil is a  very important  crop in both those countries those are  exported  and those exports are being blocked  either because there's a blockade  against ukrainian ports or because  russian  shipping can't get insurance because it  may be in danger so there's a big  shortage of supply  but there are two other ways in which um  the the supply of  food will be affected one is um in  fertilizers russia is a really important  source  of the three main kinds of fertilizer uh  and so that countries that do plant  um will will find that yields will go  down because they won't be able to  afford to use so much fertilizer and the  other is of course energy directly a  farming is an energy intensive business  you need energy to be able to run your  tractors and to be able to move the  crops around they're bulky and big you  may have prices going up very fast but  the cost of inputs are going up even  faster so even as food is running short  margins are low if this wasn't bad  enough there's one last thing to  consider which is that even before the  war started  the situation for global food supply was  looking pretty bad you had a bad weather  system la nina which affects uh both  latin america  and  and africa you've had  heat waves in india which could affect  the indian crop um and of course you had  kovid which was a big disrupt and this  comes on on top of it  and i think  you know we've spent so long talking  about this war as something that's sort  of affecting ukraine and europe and  that's rightly so but through this it  could affect hundreds of millions of the  world's most vulnerable people and all  across the world i just want to turn  within the last few minutes to a topic  which is a big topic nato membership of  sweden and finland and the consequences  thereof  shashank how does this change nato i  think it's strengthened nato this is you  know finland has the biggest artillery  force in europe this is a highly capable  armed force um that knows how to defend  itself so it's very well prepared these  aren't liabilities  i've heard some officials say that these  forces nato finnish and swedish forces  are more capable of working alongside  nato and are more capable of working  alongside it than many existing members  of the alliance that's how deep that  relationship is  look at the geography of this to defend  the baltic states latvia lithuania  estonia having finland and sweden  integrated into war plans for the baltic  region which is a single strategic and  military space is a huge advantage to  nato um so for all those reasons i think  we're looking at a really strengthened  nato um  in a way that's going to be a boon to  the alliance in the coming years  from your perspective how do you think  putin will respond to nato expansion to  include sweden and finland he thinks  that this is a good thing for him  because it it sort of reinforces his  whole argument about russia's  encirclement the whole world is against  russia uh he wants actually  nato to be there because that is indeed  what gives uh credence to everything uh  he is saying and i agree with shashank  that we are  in a going to to see a completely  different nature these are two very  strong countries  economically um you know rich countries  joining the nato um  but for putin for internal purposes i  think he will he'll exploit it uh and uh  he doesn't i think he understands that  nato is not going to attack him and he i  don't think he's not going to risk  attacking nato  i think that the u.s dynamics in this  are absolutely fascinating i mean you  started off with the americans thinking  that  um this would all be over in you know  two or three days  and and then gradually you've had this  this realization that this war could  could mean something culminating in this  um incredible package 33 billion dollars  to start with which then congress bid up  to 40 billion  and which should get through the senate  um you know at some at some point  but then there's a kind of um uh  whiplash because if indeed this war  starts to drag into 2023 and through  into 2024 when you have the presidential  campaign and if donald trump is one of  the candidates for that  i think that that gives a sense that  there's a you know there's a a stopwatch  running on this something very profound  could change in the united states  approach to this whole conflict and that  will have a big effect in europe as well  because the europeans will be very  worried about being left carrying this  thing if the americans start to back  down so um you know i i hope we will be  doing these webinars sometime towards  the end of 20 20 23 but if we will i  think that's what we'll be talking about  i'm sasha nyauta executive editor at the  economist  this was part of a digital event  produced exclusively for subscribers to  the economist if you'd like to watch the  full recording of this or any of our  other events please click the link  you'll have to register first or you can  subscribe to the economist and enjoy all  of our journalism thanks for watching  [Music]

